{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And all the days of Adam were, etc.</b> The reason Scripture needed to state both the detail and the total of the years without necessity, and did not suffice with the detail alone in the pattern Scripture follows in the portion of Noah — is that the lives of all created beings are fixed and decreed from the day of birth, as it is written (Job 14:5): ״אִם חֲרוּצִים יָמָיו״. But Adam the first man, since he was formed to live forever if he had not sinned, as we have written above, had no fixed limit. And this is what it says: <b>and all the days of Adam were which he lived</b> — the intent is not those fixed for him from the day of his coming into being, for he had no fixed limit; rather, his fixing in this world — the fixing that was made for him was אֲשֶׁר חַי (which he lived), and not אֲשֶׁר נִקְצְבוּ לוֹ (which were fixed for him). And the reason it wrote the detail and the total from Adam to Noah is to inform that all of them completed the days that were fixed for them. And the intent of Scripture in saying <b>and all the days of</b> etc. is not to tally the sum, but to say: ״וַיִּהְיוּ כָּל יְמֵי״ etc. — these are the days fixed for him from the day of his coming into being, and they did not fix for him more from the supernal world. And it did not say ״אֲשֶׁר חַי״, so that you should understand that the reckoning of what he lived is counted in the manner it wrote concerning Adam, as I have explained — and understand."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:39:22.138381866Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}